WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.645
We can't tell if our RAG is getting better

00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:05.439
or just sounding better.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:14.267
Most teams measure their RAG with thumbs up, a model grading another model, and a handful of demo questions.

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:17.341
None of those answers the question that matters.

00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:21.269
Did we stop giving wrong answers, without losing right ones?

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:30.058
QualityRag measures against recorded cases, labelled by a person: this one should answer, this one should not.

00:00:30.358 --> 00:00:38.423
Replay them under two policies. Version one answers everything it should, but admits sixty percent of the cases it shouldn't.

00:00:38.723 --> 00:00:43.929
Version one point one admits none of them, and still answers eighty-three percent.

00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:49.776
Two numbers you can track every release: right answers given, and wrong answers admitted.

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:53.310
Measure the policy, not the vibe.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:01:03.467
Replay re-evaluates recorded evidence. It doesn't re-run retrieval, and it can't label your cases. A person decides what each case should do.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:06.818
Next: why the demo looked better than production.
